    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (76.01) and **Tyler Freeman** (72.53) represent our strongest offensive engines. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a favorable matchup against Lugo, with favorable wind conditions at his back adding significant power upside. Meanwhile, **Tyler Freeman** is our most efficient play; despite a lower base projection, he catches a prime park factor (+14%) and an advantageous opponent xERA of 4.40, placing him well clear of the rest of the pack in terms of daily utility. We are also tracking **Bryson Stott** (63.53) and **Lawrence Butler** (63.21) closely; both are riding 13% platoon boosts today, making them essential cogs as we prioritize volume in the SB category against favorable defensive setups.\n\nThe toughest call today was keeping **Jonathan Aranda** (52.24) and **Masyn Winn** (50.23) in the lineup while maintaining our daily cap discipline. While **Masyn Winn** is suppressed slightly by the presence of a pitcher with high hold/pickoff efficiency, his raw floor remains sufficient to start over our available bench options. We are currently navigating a depleted bench as **Cal Raleigh**, **Will Smith**, and **Matt Chapman** are all scheduled for off-days, and **Yainer Diaz** remains sidelined on the IL. With **Daulton Varsho**, **Luis Arraez**, **Brendan Donovan**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, **Gleyber Torres**, **Wenceel Perez**, **Gavin Sheets**, and **Drew Gilbert** also unavailable for various reasons, our depth is severely tested; consequently, we are starting **Jeremiah Jackson** (42.75) despite his marginal score, simply to ensure we don't surrender potential production in the SB category.\n\nManagers should monitor **Taylor Ward**, who at 46.27 is hovering near the lower threshold for a \"marginal\" start. His matchup against Meyer is statistically difficult, and the negative platoon and park factors create a narrow margin for error. We are committed to these starters to maximize our exposure to stolen bases, but the lack of healthy pinch-hit depth on the bench means we are locked into these outcomes regardless of early-inning defensive substitutions or weather shifts.",
